{"pair_id": "train-d_loudness-5382", "original": "Three agents share one build directory. On Tuesday one of them committed a helper with no caller yet, and the next build, run by a different agent, stopped on the dead-code lint. The lint is denied at workspace level, so the error is real and reproducible. ⟦The denied lint guards the shared build against half-wired code, because whoever leaves a stub behind is told at once.⟧ The log line carries a file and line but no author, and the peer who hit it had no way to reach whoever wrote the helper. Reverting the helper turned the build green on the next run.", "substituted": "Three agents share one build directory. On Tuesday one of them committed a helper with no caller yet, and the next build, run by a different agent, stopped on the dead-code lint. The lint is denied at workspace level, so the error is real and reproducible. ⟦The denied lint stops the shared build on half-wired code, but the message lands on whichever peer builds next.⟧ The log line carries a file and line but no author, and the peer who hit it had no way to reach whoever wrote the helper. Reverting the helper turned the build green on the next run."}
{"pair_id": "train-d_loudness-6957", "original": "Reproduced in a clean ubuntu container on 2026-09-24, first attempt, with the same i386 architecture added and the same pinned Wine repository. The lane's install command exits 100 with the identical apt error, so the failure is tied neither to the runner image nor to leftover state. The CI step, pulled out of the workflow file and run with a stubbed sudo, also returned exit 100. The lane is marked continue-on-error, and the merge gate lists only the unit-test jobs as required. A red result there notifies no one, because the lane's status feeds no chat channel and no dashboard. ⟦Its exit-100 check therefore protects the release branch from a broken Wine install.⟧", "substituted": "Reproduced in a clean ubuntu container on 2026-09-24, first attempt, with the same i386 architecture added and the same pinned Wine repository. The lane's install command exits 100 with the identical apt error, so the failure is tied neither to the runner image nor to leftover state. The CI step, pulled out of the workflow file and run with a stubbed sudo, also returned exit 100. The lane is marked continue-on-error, and the merge gate lists only the unit-test jobs as required. A red result there notifies no one, because the lane's status feeds no chat channel and no dashboard. ⟦Its exit-100 check therefore only colors the lane red, and no merge decision or person depends on that.⟧"}
{"pair_id": "train-d_loudness-6407", "original": "The gate on output length is inverted relative to need. A count query over the marker returns a bare zero and a newline, two bytes, so the predicate is false and control takes the short-output branch, skipping the stderr entirely. A query that dumps 10 KB of hits receives the whole stderr. ⟦A failed count query is therefore covered, because its error is recorded in the stored stderr.⟧ The stream is stored, but the only code that reads it sits behind the same predicate, and no other reader exists. The person running the count sees a zero and cannot tell absence from loss.", "substituted": "The gate on output length is inverted relative to need. A count query over the marker returns a bare zero and a newline, two bytes, so the predicate is false and control takes the short-output branch, skipping the stderr entirely. A query that dumps 10 KB of hits receives the whole stderr. ⟦A failed count query is therefore uncovered, because its recorded stderr is never shown to the reader.⟧ The stream is stored, but the only code that reads it sits behind the same predicate, and no other reader exists. The person running the count sees a zero and cannot tell absence from loss."}
{"pair_id": "train-d_loudness-336", "original": "The eval compares four arms on adoption of result-carried guidance and on resistance to forged in-content directives. P1 predicts ADOPTION(B) > ADOPTION(A), because the delegation line gives the envelope channel authority. Both alternatives are informative: a floor at zero means result bodies are too weak a channel, and a ceiling at one means the delegation line is unnecessary. For P2, the pre-registered risk is C < D, which would mean the delegation line opened an in-content hole, and in that case the wording does not ship. The harness runs each arm three times and writes scores to a results table that the analysis script reads on every run. ⟦If a forged directive is followed in arm C, a warning is printed to the harness's stderr, which is redirected to /dev/null in the batch runner, so that warning will catch any regression before ship.⟧", "substituted": "The eval compares four arms on adoption of result-carried guidance and on resistance to forged in-content directives. P1 predicts ADOPTION(B) > ADOPTION(A), because the delegation line gives the envelope channel authority. Both alternatives are informative: a floor at zero means result bodies are too weak a channel, and a ceiling at one means the delegation line is unnecessary. For P2, the pre-registered risk is C < D, which would mean the delegation line opened an in-content hole, and in that case the wording does not ship. The harness runs each arm three times and writes scores to a results table that the analysis script reads on every run. ⟦If a forged directive is followed in arm C, the harness records it as a failed row in the results table, which the analysis script reads, so that row will catch any regression before ship.⟧"}
{"pair_id": "train-d_loudness-5423", "original": "The estimate for the mutation run was off by about tenfold. I had anchored on directory totals: the main target directory is 108 G and a worktree's is 6.7 G. Those totals accumulate over every profile the repo has ever built. The actual command is a single-crate lib test with one profile and no integration binaries, and it finished in minutes. ⟦The budget is safe even if the estimate is off again, because the job timeout will catch any overrun.⟧ That timeout kills the process and writes one line to a scheduler log that nobody is subscribed to, so an overrun would surface only as missing results. Next time I will time one mutation before multiplying.", "substituted": "The estimate for the mutation run was off by about tenfold. I had anchored on directory totals: the main target directory is 108 G and a worktree's is 6.7 G. Those totals accumulate over every profile the repo has ever built. The actual command is a single-crate lib test with one profile and no integration binaries, and it finished in minutes. ⟦A timeout would end an overrun of the budget, but only silently, so the estimate itself needs the check.⟧ That timeout kills the process and writes one line to a scheduler log that nobody is subscribed to, so an overrun would surface only as missing results. Next time I will time one mutation before multiplying."}
{"pair_id": "train-d_loudness-3759", "original": "Routing by selector only works for tools that override the selector key. The trait default returns nothing, and the content operation calls the router only when a selector is present, so a tool without an override never reaches it. The librarian adapter is the one production override, and the adapter constructor wraps every librarian-crate tool, which gives those tools a selector. Memory is different: the server registers it directly, without the wrapper, and its trait impl has no override. ⟦The router logs an error when a selector fails to match any shape, so a misrouted memory call would be flagged immediately.⟧ That leaves memory outside the routed path until either the registration or the impl changes.", "substituted": "Routing by selector only works for tools that override the selector key. The trait default returns nothing, and the content operation calls the router only when a selector is present, so a tool without an override never reaches it. The librarian adapter is the one production override, and the adapter constructor wraps every librarian-crate tool, which gives those tools a selector. Memory is different: the server registers it directly, without the wrapper, and its trait impl has no override. ⟦The router logs an error when a selector fails to match any shape, but memory supplies no selector, so that log line never runs for it.⟧ That leaves memory outside the routed path until either the registration or the impl changes."}
